**定理 8.1（preclosure numerical cost and compact entry）。** For the canonical archive pipeline of §7, let $A_*=1+h+K$. The maximum requested precision, the total canonical reply bits and the numerical peak before closure are $O(A_*)$; there are $O(\log(A_*+1))$ calls and that many outbound bits under its fixed-discovery-token contract. A conservative downstream bit-time bound is $O(A_*^5)$, apart from supplier latency and production. After allowed preclosure compression and erasure, the closed numerical peak is $O(B_*)$, not $O(A_*)$.

**证明。** Lemma 7.2 gives $b_c=O(h+1)$ because $D\ge2^{-h}$. Further $P=O(K+\log(h+K+2))$, so $b_f=O(A_*)$. The discovery precisions sum to less than $2b_c$; adding the one final request preserves $O(A_*)$. A canonical reply has length at most
$18b+2\log_2(b+1)+80$, so total returned bits have the same bound. Discovery uses $O(\log(b_c+1))$ fixed tokens; the final gamma field for $b_f$ has $O(\log(A_*+1))$ bits. The archive's changing precision and all its work are paid separately.

A constant number of $O(A_*)$-bit integer/rational fields suffices for clips, outward intervals, seeds, common-denominator direction calculations, scale comparisons and output compression. Integer-square normalization searches use $O(S)$ iterations on $O(A_*)$-bit products; all arithmetic has polynomial bit time. Summing the discovery work and final computations gives the generous fifth-power bound with linear peak scratch. This is an encoder cost for already supplied valid names, not a production bound on exact real measurements. The allowed compact entry at closure is exactly the small word and Theorem 5.1's counted machine state.

